[{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#APIs","page":"APIs","text":"The main functions are\n\nspin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue\nspin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic\nspin_weighted_spherical_eigenvalue\nspin_weighted_spherical_harmonic\n\nand their return types use\n\nSpectralDecompositionInputParams\nSpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction\nSpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction","title":"APIs"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#Complex-frequencies","page":"APIs","text":"c = 1.0 - 4.0im\nlambda = spin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue(-2, 2, 2, c)\nS = spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(-2, 2, 2, c)\nS(1.1, 0.3)\nS(1.1, 0.3; theta_derivative=1)\n\nTo follow a mode along an ordered path, pass the complex points to track_angular_mode. The returned states include intermediate points.\n\npath = [0.0, 0.5 - 2.0im, 1.0 - 4.0im]\nresult = track_angular_mode(-2, 2, 2, path)\npair = last(result.states)\nlambda = pair.lambda\nS = spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(pair)\n\nUse backend=:dense with spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic or track_angular_mode to compare with the default backend along the same path.","title":"Complex frequencies"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#Functions","page":"APIs","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#Types","page":"APIs","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#SpectralDecompositionInputParams","page":"APIs","text":"This is a composite struct type that stores the input parameters for the spectral decomposition  of a spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic\n\nfield \ns spin weight s\nl harmonic index ell\nm azimuthal index m\nc spheroidicity (c = aomega in the context of BHPT)\nN number of terms to use in the spectral decomposition","title":"SpectralDecompositionInputParams"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction","page":"APIs","text":"This is a composite struct type that stores the output from spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic\n\ntip: Tip\nSpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction(theta, phi) will return the value of the harmonic at the  coordinate (theta phi). For more details, see SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction\n\nfield \nparams a SpectralDecompositionInputParams object storing the input parameters for the spectral decomposition\ncoeffs spectral decomposition coefficients\nspherical_harmonics_l an array of SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction used in the spectral decomposition\nnormalization_const normalization constant to be divided to ensure the normalization convention is satisfied\nmethod the method used to solve for the harmonic\nlambda spin-weighted spheroidal eigenvalue lambda","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction"},{"category":"section","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction","page":"APIs","text":"This is a composite struct type that stores information about a spin-weighted spherical harmonic\n\ntip: Tip\nSpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(theta, phi) will return the value of the harmonic at the  coordinate (theta phi). For more details, see SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction\n\nfield \ns spin weight s\nl harmonic index ell\nm azimuthal index m\nlambda spin-weighted spherical eigenvalue lambda\nmethod the method used to solve for the harmonic\nchebyshev_solution numerical solution expressed in Chebyshev polynomials","title":"SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue","page":"APIs","text":"spin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue(s::Int, l::Int, m::Int, c; N::Int=-1)\n\nCompute the eigenvalue of the spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic with spin weight s, harmonic index l, azimuthal index m, and spheroidicity c (c = aomega).\n\nThe optional argument N specifies the number of terms to use in the spectral decomposition. The default value is N=-1, which indicates that a suitable value of N will be determined automatically.\n\nThis function is simply a wrapper to Teukolsky_lambda_const for backward compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue"},{"category":"function","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic","page":"APIs","text":"spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(s::Int, l::Int, m::Int, c; N::Int=-1, method=\"auto\", backend=\"auto\")\n\nConstruct the spectral decomposition of this spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic of  spin weight s, harmonic index l, azimuthal index m, and spheroidicity c (c = aomega)  using N spin-weighted spherical harmonics.\n\nNote that the default value for N=-1 indicates that a suitable value of N will be determined automatically.\n\nReturn a SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction object that can be evaluated at any point.\n\nThe method options are \"auto\" (default), \"direct\", \"jacobi\", and \"chebyshev\". Method names are case-insensitive.\n\nThe backend argument controls how the spectral decomposition is solved:\n\n\"auto\" (default): \"banded\" for real c; for complex c, follow the mode along the path with Newton steps, falling back to a full dense eigendecomposition when a step is rejected,\n\"banded\": real c only. Solve for the requested eigenpair with a banded solver, increasing N until it converges, which avoids a full dense eigendecomposition,\n\"dense\": a full dense eigendecomposition. For real c the mode is picked by its position among the sorted eigenvalues; for complex c it is followed along the path, keeping the eigenvector with the largest overlap with the previous step.\n\nBackend names are case-insensitive.\n\nFor complex c, \"mode l\" is the mode that connects continuously to the spherical harmonic l along the straight path from c = 0. To follow a different path, use track_angular_mode and pass a returned eigenpair to spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic"},{"category":"type","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction","page":"APIs","text":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction(theta, phi; theta_derivative::Int=0, phi_derivative::Int=0)\n\nCompute the value of the spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic at the point (theta, phi).  Additionally compute the theta_derivative-th derivative with respect to theta and the phi_derivative-th derivative with respect to phi exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spherical_eigenvalue","page":"APIs","text":"spin_weighted_spherical_eigenvalue(s::Int, l::Int, m::Int=0)\n\nCompute the eigenvalue of the spin-weighted spherical harmonic with spin weight s, harmonic index l, and azimuthal index m (but the eigenvalue is independent of m).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spherical_eigenvalue"},{"category":"function","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spherical_harmonic","page":"APIs","text":"spin_weighted_spherical_harmonic(s::Int, l::Int, m::Int; method=\"auto\")\n\nConstruct the spin-weighted spherical harmonic of  spin weight s, harmonic index l, and azimuthal index m.\n\nReturn a SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction object that can be evaluated at any point.\n\nBy default, the method to compute the value is chosen automatically based on the value of the harmonic index l. When l < 30, the direct evaluation method (method=\"direct\") is used where we evaluate the exact analytical solution as shown in Eq. (A8). When l >= 30, we use a Jacobi polynomial recurrence (method=\"jacobi\") for better high-l stability and speed (see https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.03691). The Chebyshev pseudo-spectral method (method=\"chebyshev\") remains available as an explicit option. Method names are case-insensitive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.spin_weighted_spherical_harmonic"},{"category":"type","location":"APIs/#SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction","page":"APIs","text":"SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(theta, phi; theta_derivative::Int=0, phi_derivative::Int=0)\n\nCompute the value of the spin-weighted spherical harmonic at the point (theta, phi). Additionally compute the theta_derivative-th derivative with respect to theta and the phi_derivative-th derivative with respect to phi exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Example-1:-Solving-and-visualizing-a-spin-weighted-spheroidal-harmonic","page":"Examples","text":"In this example, we solve for the spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic with s=-2 ell =2 m = 2 c equiv aomega = 035.\n\nusing SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\nusing Plots, LaTeXStrings\n\ns=-2; l=2; m=2; a=0.7; omega=0.5;\nswsh = spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(s, l, m, a*omega)\n\nThat's it! Now let us visualize the harmonic itself and the first and second partial derivative with respect to theta:\n\nthetas = collect(0:0.01:1)\nplot(thetas, [real(swsh(theta*pi, 0)) for theta in thetas], linewidth=2, label=L\"{}_{-2}S_{2,2}(\\theta, 0; c = 0.35)\", dpi=300)\nplot!(thetas, [real(swsh(theta*pi, 0; theta_derivative=1)) for theta in thetas], linewidth=2, label=L\"\\partial_{\\theta} {}_{-2}S_{2,2}(\\theta, 0; c = 0.35)\")\nplot!(thetas, [real(swsh(theta*pi, 0; theta_derivative=2)) for theta in thetas], linewidth=2, label=L\"\\partial_{\\theta}^2 {}_{-2}S_{2,2}(\\theta, 0; c = 0.35)\")\nplot!(\n    legendfontsize=14,\n    xguidefontsize=14,\n    yguidefontsize=14,\n    xtickfontsize=14,\n    ytickfontsize=14,\n    foreground_color_legend=nothing,\n    background_color_legend=nothing,\n    legend=:bottomright,\n    formatter=:latex,\n    xlabel=L\"\\theta/\\pi\",\n    left_margin = 2Plots.mm,\n    right_margin = 3Plots.mm,\n)\n\n(Image: SWSH.png)","title":"Example 1: Solving and visualizing a spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Example-2:-Solving-and-visualizing-a-spin-weighted-spherical-harmonic","page":"Examples","text":"In this example, we solve for the spin-weighted spherical harmonic with s=-2 ell =200 m = 10. The highly oscillatory nature could be challenging for some codes. Let us see if this is the case here. \n\nusing SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\nusing Plots, LaTeXStrings\n\ns=-2; l=200; m=10;\nY = spin_weighted_spherical_harmonic(s, l, m)\n\nNow we visualize the harmonic:\n\nthetas = collect(0:0.001:1)\nplot(thetas, [real(Y(float(theta*π), 0)) for theta in thetas], linewidth=2, label=nothing, dpi=300)\nylabel!(L\"{}_{-2}Y_{200,10}(\\theta, 0)\")\nplot!(\n    legendfontsize=14,\n    xguidefontsize=14,\n    yguidefontsize=14,\n    xtickfontsize=14,\n    ytickfontsize=14,\n    foreground_color_legend=nothing,\n    background_color_legend=nothing,\n    legend=:bottomright,\n    formatter=:latex,\n    xlabel=L\"\\theta/\\pi\",\n    left_margin = 2Plots.mm,\n    right_margin = 3Plots.mm,\n)\n\n(Image: Y.png) Looking good!","title":"Example 2: Solving and visualizing a spin-weighted spherical harmonic"},{"category":"section","location":"#Home","page":"Home","text":"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.jl computes spin-weighted spheroidal harmonics and eigenvalues using a spectral decomposition method.\n\nThe two main features are implemented as\n\nspin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic for computing the harmonic _s S_ell m(theta phi c equiv a omega), and\nspin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue for computing the eigenvalue\n\nand both supporting complex spheroidicity c (and hence complex frequency omega). See Quick-start below for some simple examples.\n\nIn particular, we use the following normalization convention:\n\n    int_0^pi left _s S_ell m(theta c) right^2 sin(theta)  dtheta = frac12pi  \n\nidentical to the convention used in the Mathematica package SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics from the Black Hole Perturbation Toolkit.\n\nAdditionally, we provide two similar functions\n\nspin_weighted_spherical_harmonic _s Y_ell m(theta phi), and\nspin_weighted_spherical_eigenvalue\n\nthat return the exact harmonic and eigenvalue respectively.\n\nExact partial derivatives (with respect to either theta and/or phi) can be evaluated by specifying the derivative order with theta_derivative and phi_derivative respectively when calling the functions for a harmonic.","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"To install the package using the Julia package manager, simply type the following in the Julia REPL:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Quick-start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Computing-the-spin-weighted-spheroidal-eigenvalue","page":"Home","text":"For example, to compute the spin-weighted spheroidal eigenvalue lambda for the mode s = -2 ell = 2 m = 2 a = 07 omega = 05, simply do\n\njulia> using SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\njulia> s=-2; l=2; m=2; a=0.7; omega=0.5;\njulia> spin_weighted_spheroidal_eigenvalue(s, l, m, a*omega)\n1.6966094016353415","title":"Computing the spin-weighted spheroidal eigenvalue"},{"category":"section","location":"#Computing-the-spin-weighted-spheroidal-harmonic","page":"Home","text":"For example, to compute the spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic for the mode s = -2 ell = 2 m = 2 a = 07 omega = 05 at theta = pi6 phi = pi3, simply do\n\njulia> using SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\njulia> s=-2; l=2; m=2; a=0.7; omega=0.5;\njulia> theta=π/6; phi=π/3;\n       # Construct the SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction\njulia> swsh = spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(s, l, m, a*omega)\nSpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction(s = -2, l = 2, m = 2, c = 0.35, method = spectral, lambda = 1.6966094016353415)\njulia> swsh(theta, phi)\n-0.2869893540048821 + 0.49708014236782677im","title":"Computing the spin-weighted spheroidal harmonic"},{"category":"section","location":"#Computing-the-spherical-spheroidal-mixing-coefficients","page":"Home","text":"For example, to compute the spherical-spheroidal mixing coefficients for the mode s = -2 ell = 2 m = 2 a = 07 omega = 05, simply do\n\njulia> using SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics\njulia> s=-2; l=2; m=2; a=0.7; omega=0.5;\n       # Construct the SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction\njulia> swsh = spin_weighted_spheroidal_harmonic(s, l, m, a*omega)\n       # swsh.spherical_harmonics_l lists which spherical harmonic \\ell modes were used in the decomposition\n       # swsh.coeffs lists the mixing coefficients for each of the modes\nSpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicFunction(s = -2, l = 2, m = 2, c = 0.35, method = spectral, lambda = 1.6966094016353415)\njulia> swsh.spherical_harmonics_l, swsh.coeffs\n(Union{Nothing, SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction}[SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 2, 2, 4, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 3, 2, 10, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 4, 2, 18, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 5, 2, 28, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 6, 2, 40, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 7, 2, 54, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 8, 2, 70, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 9, 2, 88, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 10, 2, 108, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 11, 2, 130, :direct, nothing), SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonics.SpinWeightedSphericalHarmonicFunction(-2, 12, 2, 154, :direct, nothing), nothing, nothing, nothing, nothing, nothing], ComplexF64[0.9978175786395768 + 0.0im, 0.06594444546903164 + 0.0im, 0.0033751518839967 + 0.0im, 0.00013490589879448426 + 0.0im, 4.447157422387261e-6 + 0.0im, 1.2390017730232787e-7 + 0.0im, 2.992937317706995e-9 + 0.0im, 6.367454586278411e-11 + 0.0im, 1.2108652048083582e-12 + 0.0im, 2.0797457254151272e-14 + 0.0im, 3.2574964414017216e-16 + 0.0im, 4.687056456089269e-18 + 0.0im, 6.237165963082467e-20 + 0.0im, 7.717822512359198e-22 + 0.0im, 8.924593710959042e-24 + 0.0im, 9.684081521631236e-26 + 0.0im])","title":"Computing the spherical-spheroidal mixing coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"The package is licensed under the MIT License.","title":"License"}]
